WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.568
This would be the start of a very, very interesting topic, which is synchronicity and astrology through the lens of Jung. Jung did not come up with this concept, but pretty much wrote a lot about them and thought a lot about them.

00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:37.519
And a little bit background knowledge to Jung himself, famous Swiss psychologist, lived from 1875 till 1961. And had a very long life, very productive life, wrote an incredible amount of books and wrote a very, very wide array of topics.

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:52.307
So when you, for those who look into the group and for those who looked into the podcast, see that there are very, very different topics that Jung talks about, but he connects them all pretty well through the psyche and his investigations and his concepts.

00:00:53.287 --> 00:01:16.267
And something was pretty special about Jung because he was there at the beginning of psychology when it all started as a science, beginning of the 20th century, and especially psychoanalysis, which was started by Freud. And when Jung was very young, he was a little bit like a mythbuster.

00:01:17.278 --> 00:01:40.915
So he would look at different phenomena. And would find an explanation for it, which was often the unconscious. So he wrote his PhD thesis, for example, about a cousin of his who was a medium. So she would talk in foreign tongues of the different personalities.

00:01:41.027 --> 00:02:05.663
This was a huge craze at that time to have séances with mediums that then would change the characters and have strange knowledge and so on. So he would write his PhD thesis about her and taking part at the séances and really watching what was happening and recording and trying to find commonalities with other mediums.

00:02:06.964 --> 00:02:28.896
And he basically came to the conclusion that she was not really aware of doing something. But that does not mean that she was not part of it. He pretty much said that the, she, the person as a medium would be split in a conscious part and an unconscious part.

00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:46.937
And the unconscious part would do things and the conscious part would just not recognize them, that the person would be doing it. It's a little bit when you are thinking about something and your facial expression changes. Other people can see what you think and what your mood is and so on. It happens automatically. It's unconscious.

00:02:47.514 --> 00:03:06.936
You don't know what your face looks like unless you have a mirror around or people tell you what you're looking like. And this, in a more extreme form, he saw in the mediums, especially when things are moving. He realized that it was a person moving the table or objects, but the person did not realize that it would do these movements.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:23.999
So it looked Like things were moving by their own. And he would look at different phenomena and see this unconscious part. And this is very often the explanation for him. Okay, something was happening, but people were just not conscious of it.

00:03:25.106 --> 00:03:40.285
Same, he visited a friend in his summer house and suddenly he would have visions of a dead person next to him in the dark. So he had a feeling that there was some specter, that the house was haunted.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:04:01.701
And he talked to the owner and he said, yeah, this many guests have this feeling in this room, but only in this room in the house where he was staying, Jung. And through investigation from the previous owner of the house, he found out that somebody has died in this room.

00:04:03.112 --> 00:04:26.941
So this would be a little bit spiritual, like, oh, the ghost of the person is still in the room. But Jung's suspicion was rather that there was still some blood or some matter of the person still there, and that his nose would pick up on this. So the unconscious would register these very low signal information, which could not reach consciousness.

00:04:27.262 --> 00:04:50.543
And what the person would encounter would not really be a ghost or specter, but rather the unconscious experience of something. So it's really that in the psyche, there are different alleys of perception. And the conscious perception needs very loud noises, but the unconscious can deal with lower noises.

00:04:51.303 --> 00:05:11.351
But the unconscious talks to the consciousness. And this way these visions come because it's correct. Somebody died there. So he had a vision of a dead woman. So the connection is there. And this is Jung in his, let's say, mythbuster phase where he takes things, looks at them and says, okay, there's a psychological explanation for it.

00:05:11.576 --> 00:05:16.955
And he was a psychologist, trained psychologist, worked as a psychotherapist and was really involved in that.

00:05:18.303 --> 00:05:42.260
But As long as he's been living and the more he investigated, the more he realized, okay, there is actually something more going on than just this explanation of, ah, it's the unconscious and people are not realizing that they're experiencing or that they're doing something, that the things are happening around them, but they're just not aware of.

00:05:43.705 --> 00:06:01.532
So He looked at all those different things. So psychology, of course, but he looked also at alchemy, astrology, synchronicity, and these effects and events to find why are people interested in them and what is the psychological value.

00:06:01.725 --> 00:06:25.097
Because for him, everything that humans did and were interested in were a sign that this is how the psyche functions. Something profound, right? Same. And when you have things that are popular, like songs or movies or books or anything else, it has to speak to something inside a lot of human beings.

00:06:25.754 --> 00:06:49.721
And this must signify something about human experience and how we are structured inside, because it's difficult to look into the psyche because the psyche is looking at itself and tries to interpret itself, so it's prone to all of the different biases that are possibly available. So one topic he really looked into was synchronicity.

00:06:50.672 --> 00:07:14.195
And synchronicity, I got, I had a hard time to understand it. He had a very good example that I found later in a letter that he wrote to someone. Because there's a linking connection between causality and synchronicity. And causality is just this influenced this, and this influenced this, this influenced this.

00:07:14.644 --> 00:07:34.856
So there's this logical chain of events, and one thing happens after the other in a temporal order. And as an example would be when you would go to the beach and standing there looking at a river or the sea or whatever, and a hat would be lying there without much else.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:08:01.115
The causality way of looking at it would be, okay, why is this hat here? How did it come here? And his example would go that, ah, okay, there's a boat on the river and there are people on there. Likely the wind came, blown off the hat from the person, and it's now here on the beach. And this explains why the hat is here. But a synchronistic approach would be looking at it.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:24.549
It's not really this, okay, why is something here and what happened before, but rather what is the meaning of all those different things happening at this exact point in time? This is not only, okay, why is this, not why is this hat here, but what does it mean that this hat is here, that I am here, that it's this time of the day.

00:08:24.983 --> 00:08:43.295
That there's a couple in the bushes that are kissing, that there's a rotten tree next to me, that there are birds flying over. So the exact moment with all its components, what does it signify that all these things come together at this specific point in time?

00:08:45.272 --> 00:09:01.584
And a lot of, um, foretelling. Techniques like the I Ching or tarot work this way. For those who don't know, the I Ching is basically a couple of sticks.

00:09:01.954 --> 00:09:24.770
It's an old Chinese technique and you have a huge bundle of them, of the sticks, and you put them like this on the table and you just let go and then they fall in all different directions and create a certain pattern. And then there's this huge book called the I Ching, and you would look up, okay, the pattern that the sticks formed, what does it signify?

00:09:26.506 --> 00:09:43.858
And this would be synchronistic in that the sticks are corresponding to this point in time, signify something about this point of time, and telling something about other things that are causally not related, but rather happening at the same time.

00:09:44.372 --> 00:10:07.907
This is why you can pick up this stick and let it fall, and it's every time different because the point in time changes. And tarot works also this way that you, for example, you shuffle the cards and when a card falls out, then you have to take this card. Or that you lay the cards for, there are different techniques to do it.

00:10:08.292 --> 00:10:29.164
But again, this idea of, okay, I have a question for the cards. So I draw the cards, I look at the cards, and these are telling something again about this specific point in time. And I can't really replicate this point in time. It's like a river. You can't step in the same river twice.

00:10:29.405 --> 00:10:52.889
There's a proverb about this because the river has already moved on. Same with time. So you can't put out the sticks and 5 times after the other, the same pattern will be, otherwise we will have 5 times the same set of cards. Because through your knowledge and through the changes of time, of course, this has to be different in a synchronistic view.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:11:16.375
And this connection between causality and synchronicity fits into Jung's pattern of shadow, which means that shadow are the things that are not illuminated. This means when you concentrate on one thing, you have to ignore a lot of other things that are happening at the same time.

00:11:16.456 --> 00:11:35.903
His idea was that reality and the universe and everything that's happening is so complex and humans are very, very limited that they have to focus on very specific points to experience something. So we can only experience a part and have to ignore everything of the rest. And that would be shadow, the shadow parts.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:52.988
And basically you have to look around in all directions to get a complete view, but you can't have the complete view in just one picture. And the same way as causality describes this logical progression of things.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:12:17.456
Synchronicity is not going through time, but rather autonomous to it by taking this slice of time and saying these things are not causally related, but they are temporal related in this specific point in time. And he says, okay, this causality idea and thinking, it's a very Western thing.

00:12:19.512 --> 00:12:42.300
And it's so old and so natural, especially in the Western world, that we don't really think about other ways. But he described it in the East, China, for example, that this synchronistic view is much more common to have, and especially with Tao, this concept of Tao, to say, okay, what does it mean that all these things are now happening at the same time?

00:12:43.663 --> 00:13:08.586
So synchronicity, something which is so specific that it very likely only happens one time, which makes it difficult to prove, right? Because when you look at science, it is very causality-wise. So when you want to prove something scientifically, you need to be able to repeat it and look at it statistically to find the pattern.

00:13:08.666 --> 00:13:25.443
You have to be able to replicate it. This is something very important. When you have a discovery, people will try to replicate it to see whether it's true or not. But when you have something that is so specific that it can only happen one time, it's not really scientific.

00:13:26.026 --> 00:13:45.770
And that makes it, that makes it difficult to see these synchronistic events and match them. And He came up with this idea and investigated it when he was a professor in Basel.

00:13:46.219 --> 00:14:03.209
So after he studied, Jung, he taught as a professor in Basel and he met, of course, other professors who were there. And one of them was Albert Einstein. And they sat down, Jung wrote in a letter and talked about their different fields of expertise.

00:14:03.707 --> 00:14:24.543
And this was before Einstein talked about relativity publicly, but he was already investigating this relative behavior of time and space. And he was trying to explain it to Jung, and Jung was completely dumbfounded because it's physics and it's very complicated physics.

00:14:26.285 --> 00:14:36.107
And Jung came a little bit at ease when it was his turn to explain his field of expertise. Because Einstein had very big problems with psychology.

00:14:37.455 --> 00:14:53.882
But Jung said this was already like a germ in the back of his mind, that there's something that is not really static about space and especially time, and that there's more things going on than we normally think about in a causal way.

00:14:53.962 --> 00:15:16.593
Because what was before Einstein and these physicists was this very Newtonian worldview of causality and causal events. And there was a point in time where the idea was there that physics is completely explored and there's almost some problems that only have to be fixed and then, and investigated, and then everything can be explained.

00:15:16.641 --> 00:15:35.371
And then comes this whole point with relativity and quantum physics and so on, which makes everything way more complicated. So it was pretty much around this time. And Jung always worked on this problem over his life of relative time and space and then synchronicity.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:16:00.965
And in his later life, he met Wolfgang Pauli, the famous physics Nobel Prize winner, who was a patient of his, which was a secret for a very long time, but became then public. And there's a very good book about the Dreams of Pauli. Called Dream Symbols of the Individuation Process, which I can highly recommend. It's a great seminar about individuation and dreams.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:12.834
And they formulated more this idea about synchronicity and what it could mean and investigated it. So Jung's whole life was about this.

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:31.013
And his idea is really centered around this idea of time, that the unconscious and the psyche has a very strong idea about time and the passing of time.

00:16:32.105 --> 00:16:52.082
He had this example of you can make yourself wake up at specific times, even when there's no clock around to wake you up, but also to have this constant changing of the seasons with the corresponding behavior, change of plants and animals and so on.

00:16:52.678 --> 00:17:16.997
So that there's something inside the psyche which is always ticking and bringing in certain changes seemingly all by itself. And he had this idea of archetypal influence. So, okay, I have to first talk a little bit of archetypes.

00:17:17.078 --> 00:17:36.271
When you have the psyche and you have a consciousness, you have first your personal unconscious. This is really this shadow part, the things that you don't attend to about your own psyche. But even lower than that is the collective unconscious, which is basically human nature. This is the part that makes us similar to all the other human beings that are around.

00:17:37.041 --> 00:18:01.388
And even deeper than that, that makes us similar to certain animals and so on. And you go lower, lower, lower. Until you hit chemical components of the body. And of course, then in the end, physics. And the collective unconscious for Jung consisted of archetypes, which a little bit like mini dramas that are happening, that have beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:18:02.472 --> 00:18:23.759
This means certain patterns of behavior that just come up all by themselves. You can see this very strongly in Animals, as animals know how to breed and find a partner and go south when it's getting too cold. Nobody has to teach them.

00:18:24.225 --> 00:18:49.079
It's an instinct that is already in there, that is their nature to do. And these archetypes have influence over life of people because human beings do do the same things. We look for partners. We have certain goals. We have certain things to do. And we have also different stages in life where we have different requirements and different wishes what to do.

00:18:50.719 --> 00:19:15.372
So he saw this component of time really in the collective unconscious and with the archetypes. As the archetypes removed from time as they're eternal, it's already precursor for evolutionary biology, that certain things that happen so often in the past that they become part of the psyche of humans or other animals.

00:19:16.921 --> 00:19:39.121
And this, he hints at that, explains a little bit why certain events can happen at the same time, even though then they are not causally related. And that's by having an archetypal underpinning and the archetype acting up at different places in different psyches at the same time.

00:19:40.004 --> 00:19:58.071
And it's a little bit similar to clocks. So when you have one clock and you start having other clocks just connected to that, uh, think about you have a clock and you split it up now in two clocks and they have the same time or just split up, split it up, split up.

00:19:58.554 --> 00:20:18.746
It would seem like all the clocks have the same time because there's some connection between them, but they're just started at the same time. And this is the reason why they show the same time. So there's this connection of different parts, which are seemingly unrelated because the collective unconscious is coming from below.

00:20:19.247 --> 00:20:35.238
So there's not really a direct connection between things happening at the same time. But rather through a very roundabout way, through an incredibly long path, these things are related. Sorry if this sounds a little bit confusing, but it will become clear with some examples.

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:57.501
So what Carl Gustav Jung proposed to be more aware of these synchronistic events and to be a little bit more convinced of them is to look at coincidences. Things that are seemingly unrelated, but then again related somehow.

00:20:58.737 --> 00:21:18.158
There's a movie about Carl Gustav Jung and his friendship with Freud, and it's with Viggo Mortensen, Michael Fassbender, and Keira Knightley. It's called A Dangerous Method. It's a good movie, and it's based on a play which is again based on The Biography of Carl Gustav Jung.

00:21:19.867 --> 00:21:41.794
And there he very often says he does not believe in coincidences, and he does it so often that it sounds a little bit like a catchphrase. He does not say it like this in his written works, but it's actually this idea of, ah, there's not really coincidences, there's just a connection you don't really see. And I had personally this experience, which was rather synchronistic.

00:21:42.429 --> 00:22:06.378
And I want to tell it to you just to give you an example of how synchronicity is supposed to be understood in a Jungian sense, which is one day I was reading a book in the morning and it was from Fry. I forgot the first name, but it's about the retelling of the Greek hero myth. And it's very good. It's very humorous.

00:22:06.991 --> 00:22:29.834
Stephen Fry, I think. The British comedian. And in the book, in this chapter that I read, he talked about the Titan Atlas who had the shoulders, who held the sky on his shoulders. And there I learned that basically the Atlas Mountains are North Africa, which I did not know. It never occurred to me that this is the same thing.

00:22:30.911 --> 00:22:42.918
And later that day I went to work and I had to look at some candidates that wanted to fill a role in the company, and I was supposed to screen them whether they are suitable for an interview.

00:22:43.721 --> 00:23:07.083
And one of them wrote down that he had experience and of course has some hobbies, and he wrote down that he was a paragliding instructor who spent half a year in North Africa in the Atlas Mountains. And I always thought, okay, well, that's a little bit weird. I just learned today that the Atlas Mountains are in North Africa. Now I have some guy who spent half a year there.

00:23:08.111 --> 00:23:27.941
And so I worked the whole day and after work I went drinking with some work colleagues and we basically went around the city a little bit drunk and we went into some places I've never been before. So I'm completely lost. It's completely dark in the middle of the night and we're walking around and they want to bring me to some restaurant or something.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:46.100
So I have no idea where I am. Everything is dark. And I go around this corner, and right in front of us is this restaurant. And in very bright yellow letters, it's saying Atlas Mountains. I say, okay, yeah, this is super weird.

00:23:46.486 --> 00:24:03.082
And it's this kind of coincidences where Jung says, okay, there's something happening that Certain thoughts are popping up, certain things are happening around, and this is just— nothing of this is causally related, but for some reason it's happening in a very confined time frame.

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:25.174
So this is synchronicity, and it will be important for, especially with this concept of time, for the next topic of the evening, which is astrology. And for astrology, I have to apologize because I don't have so much knowledge about astrology itself.

00:24:25.415 --> 00:24:38.615
So I'm very happy to have some people here who can help out on that after the presentation. Basically, all my knowledge is rudimentary and the view that Jung had on astrology.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:25:01.794
So I apologize already at the beginning, but it's no problem to explain the concepts and to understand the concepts, even if you don't have so much knowledge about astrology. So very shortly, astrology is around the stars and the planets and how they relate to each other.

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:20.185
And the relation of them to each other can say something about things that are happening now, or that could be happening in the future. And it's these 12 signs corresponding roughly to a month in the year.

00:25:20.814 --> 00:25:43.863
And after the Earth rotates around the sun, I'm so, I'm so hoping that I'm getting this right. We are in a different sign in the horoscope, in the astrological horoscope. At the same time, you have this idea of, okay, when you are born in a certain month, you have certain characteristics, or it's saying something about your fate.

00:25:43.943 --> 00:26:03.422
So you can have a horoscope every day, every month, every year that will say something about your future, the things you have to look out for, things that are positive, things that are negative, and also how people can fit together based on their horoscope. When they were born.

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:19.666
For example, I'm born in January, late January, which means I'm Aquarius, a water man, and Wassermann, how we say in German. And this is the short introduction to astrology in general.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:38.067
There's one very interesting information, and this I didn't know, and Jung wrote about it, that there is, for a very, very long time, there were only 11 signs and not 12. And the 12th part, which is the last part, is scales.

00:26:39.464 --> 00:26:54.605
And scales, it's actually the cut-off pieces from the, from Scorpio. So, and the interesting part is it's the last thing, it's the only thing that it's not an animal or a person.

00:26:56.180 --> 00:27:12.803
So it's already moving away from this animal person part to more cultural part, civilization part, because astrology as an endeavor happened not with primitive tribes, people living in the forest.

00:27:13.256 --> 00:27:28.564
They were not really interested in the stars so much, but rather as soon as you had Civilization, you had people looking at the stars and finding, of course, certain connections.

00:27:28.757 --> 00:27:54.259
It was really this, suddenly, tracking of time, changing of months, changing of seasons, and how people became aware of the regularity, for example, of the flood in Egypt, which was very important for, of the Nile River to fertilize the lands, was just by documenting of all those lights up there in the sky and finding afterwards the patterns.

00:27:54.308 --> 00:28:18.779
So there were first the documentation and then the realization, oh, there's something in the data, a little bit like data mining that helped them then to predict certain things like the Nile. So it was not really causally related, but you have certain events that could be predicted by astrology because changing of time, such as the Nile or other things.

00:28:20.428 --> 00:28:40.709
In earlier times, it was interesting to, let's say, tribal people, was rather the moon, because the moon is a very big clock in the sky. It has a regularity and you can see how it's changing. So very often primitive societies were more interested in the moon.

00:28:41.196 --> 00:28:56.640
The more it got civilized, it was more this look into the sun, but also the look into the stars and the tracking of time through stars. And this started in Mesopotamia for the Western form of astrology.

00:28:56.929 --> 00:29:14.361
In all cultures all over the world, you have different kinds of astrology, and the one I'm talking about is this Western kind. With the 12 signs, which started in Mesopotamia and then through the Egyptians is still used till today.

00:29:16.892 --> 00:29:35.079
And here, of course, Jung as a mystic comes in and says, okay, astrology as this science is not really accurate anymore, because when we say, okay, we're looking at the stars, and see their positions and how they behave.

00:29:36.333 --> 00:29:58.515
The rotation axis of the Earth has changed in the last 4,000 years because it's always changing slightly, which of course shifts the star signs in the sky, which means everything is shifted. So the astrology that we are looking at today is rather how the sky looked like 4,000 years ago. So he said, okay, yeah.

00:29:59.612 --> 00:30:18.844
If there's a connection, it can't be what it actually is in the moment, but rather what it was 4,000 years ago. He noted that every time when he talked about astrology, that people would write to him and say like, okay, why are you so interested? And why do you try to make it so complicated?

00:30:19.652 --> 00:30:42.846
It's vibrations from the stars and the planets, and they are influencing the things here. And he said, he doesn't think it's vibrations, but there has to be something else that's going on here. And all in all, he said astrology itself is not really reliable in the sense that you cannot use it like a clock or you can use it very scientifically.

00:30:43.640 --> 00:31:06.939
For that, it's too unreliable. But he found something. That made it for him interesting again. So it's this first, it's always this mythbuster mode of, okay, I'm trying to test the concept and see, okay, it's not the obvious answers. And then he has this way of going deeper into it to find an alternative explanation.

00:31:07.698 --> 00:31:31.158
And this alternative explanation for him is rather that the sky was a kind of screen where people projected their psyche on, which means what is the sky and the planets and the stars really? It's really blinking lights.

00:31:31.351 --> 00:31:49.197
So it's, you don't have the points and say, okay, this is now Scorpio and this is, these are, those are twins and so on. It's human beings that label these phenomena. And who give names to it and pictures and so on.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:32:09.485
So for him, the act of projection is that you not really seeing the world, but rather your psyche, but you're unaware of it, which means you might have the feeling that something objective is happening, but it's rather subjective.

00:32:10.194 --> 00:32:26.510
So he believed that the people in the past looked into the sky and saw, okay, here we have certain figures, certain animals, certain objects, and we see them clearly and things happening. And we have the planets, and the planets are called Jupiter and Venus and so on.

00:32:26.575 --> 00:32:44.017
And this is no coincidence that they are the gods, as the gods are powerful and they're almighty and they are also way up high. But In the end, Jupiter does not really have a name. We've given Jupiter a name. We did not ask Jupiter, okay, what's your name? But rather people said, oh, this is Jupiter.

00:32:45.366 --> 00:33:01.021
And this is a projected part that people have things of the psyche and it's outside and they document it. And this gives insight into how the psyche works and what's happening inside in the psyche.

00:33:02.333 --> 00:33:12.837
So he saw astrology really as the first form of psychology, as people interacting with their psyche.

00:33:13.881 --> 00:33:33.331
They were thinking they're interacting with the outside world in an objective manner and even a scientific manner, an investigative manner by collecting data, by interpreting, by trying to predict and so on. But rather what they documented was the things happening in their psyche.

00:33:36.052 --> 00:33:44.506
And he would find these symbols and labels that would be corresponding for him to certain psychological phenomena.

00:33:45.231 --> 00:34:08.015
For example, that the sun has a meaning and the moon has a meaning, which is the, the moon being female and unconscious for him, representative of the unconscious as it would come out in the night when it's dark and people could not see correctly and all weird things would be happening.

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:30.480
The sun being the warm shining thing being associated more with consciousness and that these things take turns. And play different roles. And the interesting thing is this idea of lunacy and lunatics comes from Luna, which is Latin for moon.

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:55.704
So he saw this as a psychological happening, this investigation of astrology, but there's more to it, and he admitted to that, that there has to be something to this astrological endeavor, especially in context of personality.

00:34:58.040 --> 00:35:18.554
Because what Jung did very often in his practice, when he could not find clearly the personality of the person, because he said, when you have a neurosis, you're not really sure if you're interacting with the person or a complex, psychological trauma they have inside, which makes it difficult to see, okay, what is the real person? He would get a horoscope.

00:35:19.405 --> 00:35:38.591
He would get a very specific horoscope for the person to have just a starting point to find, okay, and start digging where is the real person. And he said there is something to it when people are born at certain points in the year that this has an influence on their personality. And in that way also on their lives.

00:35:39.190 --> 00:36:01.885
